WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.963
Notifications keep users informed about what happens on a site, and what needs their attention.

00:00:06.963 --> 00:00:12.749
The Notifications module stores every notification in a notification center, and delivers it

00:00:12.749 --> 00:00:16.291
by email or SMS, according to each user's preferences.

00:00:16.291 --> 00:00:21.251
In this video, we'll see the bell, the notification center, the preferences of

00:00:21.251 --> 00:00:25.915
each user, a workflow that sends notifications, and how a module uses the

00:00:25.915 --> 00:00:27.450
center as its inbox.

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:37.528
Every notification is stored in the notification center of its user, before it's delivered.

00:00:37.528 --> 00:00:41.368
The user reads it in the admin, and receives it by each method

00:00:41.368 --> 00:00:43.570
they chose, such as email or SMS.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.651
A module can use the notification center as its inbox.

00:00:47.651 --> 00:00:52.202
A notification can link to a page, and it's marked as read when it's opened.

00:00:52.202 --> 00:00:55.699
It has a type and a reference, so it can be filtered, and

00:00:55.699 --> 00:00:59.030
updated or deleted when the record it's about changes.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:04.353
Each type can have its own display, and its own delivery preferences.

00:01:04.353 --> 00:01:09.840
And a module can hide, or restrict, the notifications that a user can no longer see.

00:01:13.983 --> 00:01:18.543
The bell in the admin navbar shows how many notifications you haven't read.

00:01:18.995 --> 00:01:23.521
Open it, and each notification shows a dot while it's unread, a bold

00:01:23.521 --> 00:01:27.557
subject, a summary of two lines at most, and a line with its

00:01:27.557 --> 00:01:29.025
type and its time.

00:01:29.477 --> 00:01:32.617
A module can add actions to a notification.

00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:36.847
In this demo, a module added a Reply button to its mentions.

00:01:37.298 --> 00:01:41.296
Click the subject of a notification, and it opens the page it points

00:01:41.296 --> 00:01:43.728
to, and marks the notification as read.

00:01:44.192 --> 00:01:46.000
The count has gone down by one.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.472
The link to the Notification Center is at the bottom of the list.

00:01:53.644 --> 00:01:58.345
The Notification Center lists all your notifications, read and unread.

00:01:58.345 --> 00:02:01.994
Each one shows its type as a badge, next to its time.

00:02:02.446 --> 00:02:05.678
The Type filter lists the types that modules registered.

00:02:05.678 --> 00:02:07.556
Let's choose Approvals needed.

00:02:08.042 --> 00:02:12.466
The filter is part of the search, so a module can link straight to it.

00:02:12.466 --> 00:02:18.172
This link shows only the mentions, and the search field holds the term type, colon, Mention.

00:02:18.622 --> 00:02:23.167
When a module restricts a notification, the user sees that they no longer

00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:25.468
have access to it, and no details.

00:02:25.468 --> 00:02:28.632
A module can also hide a notification completely.

00:02:29.083 --> 00:02:33.784
A notification without a link opens its own page in the notification center,

00:02:33.784 --> 00:02:36.593
which shows its body, and marks it as read.

00:02:40.794 --> 00:02:45.044
At the bottom of the user editor, Notification Types lists one row for

00:02:45.044 --> 00:02:48.454
each type, and one column for each notification method.

00:02:48.906 --> 00:02:52.525
A module can turn a method off by default for its type.

00:02:52.525 --> 00:02:58.103
Here, Approvals needed isn't sent by email unless the user turns it on, and Mentions is.

00:02:58.103 --> 00:03:01.486
A method turned off above stays off for every type.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.133
Workflows can send notifications too.

00:03:08.133 --> 00:03:13.553
This workflow starts when an article is published, and notifies the owner of the content.

00:03:13.553 --> 00:03:16.120
Its notification has a type and a link.

00:03:16.572 --> 00:03:20.222
Let's create an article, give it a title, and publish it.

00:03:22.359 --> 00:03:26.934
The workflow runs, and a notification of the Mention type arrives in the

00:03:26.934 --> 00:03:28.459
bell, with its link.

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:37.112
This page belongs to a sample module with comments, not to Orchard Core.

00:03:37.112 --> 00:03:41.865
It plays the part of a module that sends notifications to the signed-in user.

00:03:42.316 --> 00:03:46.206
Let's send a mention, with a link and details about its project.

00:03:46.656 --> 00:03:49.365
The module can also change what it sent.

00:03:49.365 --> 00:03:55.196
Editing a comment updates its mentions, and approving everything marks the approvals as read.

00:03:55.647 --> 00:04:00.639
And deleting a comment deletes its mentions, so no notification points at a

00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:02.797
record that no longer exists.

00:04:06.997 --> 00:04:10.968
A module registers its types in its startup, with a display name, a

00:04:10.968 --> 00:04:14.177
description, and the methods that are off by default.

00:04:14.927 --> 00:04:18.337
Then it sends a message with a URL, a type, and a reference

00:04:18.337 --> 00:04:19.964
to the record it's about.

00:04:19.964 --> 00:04:23.427
Data for the display of the type is stored with the message.

00:04:24.177 --> 00:04:29.345
To keep notifications in sync with the record, the module uses the notification

00:04:29.345 --> 00:04:34.757
manager, to list, update, mark as read, or delete the notifications of a reference.

00:04:36.757 --> 00:04:41.630
The Notifications module gives users one place for what needs their attention, and

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:44.177
gives your modules an inbox to build on.

00:04:44.177 --> 00:04:45.617
Thanks for watching.

